    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and then \\(a + b\\), we need to determine the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\) and the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\).\n\n1. **Find \\(a\\):**\n   - The smallest two-digit number is \\(10\\).\n   - To find the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\), we divide \\(10\\) by \\(3\\) and round up to the next whole number.\n   \\[\n   10 \\div 3 \\approx 3.33\n   \\]\n   - Rounding \\(3.33\\) up gives \\(4\\).\n   - Thus, \\(4 \\times 3 = 12\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(a = 12\\).\n\n2. **Find \\(b\\):**\n   - The smallest three-digit number is \\(100\\).\n   - To find the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\), we divide \\(100\\) by \\(4\\) and round up to the next whole number.\n   \\[\n   100 \\div 4 = 25\n   \\]\n   - Thus, \\(25 \\times 4 = 100\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(b = 100\\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\(a + b\\):**\n   \\[\n   a + b = 12 + 100 = 112\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{112}\\).",
